@startuml
!include theme.iuml
title Analysis statistics — all saved snapshots, one page of summaries
hide footbox
autonumber
participant "FrancAnalyzer / Analysis\n<<browser functions>>" as UI
participant "analyzer_api.test_report()" as API
participant "analyzer_history.test_report()" as History
participant "store : PublicWorkspaceStore" as Store
participant "VocabularyApproval\n<<model class>>" as Approval
activate UI
UI -> API : GET /api/analyzer/tests?offset=0&limit=25
activate API
note over API : Public read allowlist and rate limit passed;\ncourse_operation() holds the shared lock.
API -> History : test_report(offset, limit)
activate History
History -> Store : iter_analyzer_tests()
activate Store
Store --> History : iterator of validated RecordedTest snapshots
deactivate Store
loop each saved snapshot, including intentional repeated tests
  History -> Approval : from_statistics(record.lexical.statistics)
  activate Approval
  Approval --> History : UNKNOWN-only approval
  deactivate Approval
  note over History : Aggregate saved raw/normalized tokens, categories, variations,\nunknown review, vocabulary/CFG totals and recorded metadata.
  opt this record is inside the requested summary page
    History -> Store : ownership("analyzer_test", record.id)\nvia record_ownership()
    activate Store
    Store --> History : redacted Ownership
    deactivate Store
    note over History : Construct OwnedTestSummary, not the full snapshot.
  end
end
History --> API : TestReport(all totals/statistics, paged tests)
deactivate History
API --> UI : report with separate summary and grammar_summary
deactivate API
note over UI : Inspecting a selected test uses GET /api/analyzer/tests/{id}.\nNothing is reclassified against a newly edited Collection or CFG.
deactivate UI
legend bottom
  Iteration is lazy: the store validates SQL rows as consumed, retaining one complete snapshot at a time.
  offset/limit page only summaries, not the population underlying the aggregates.
  Empty acceptance rates are null; historical test counts are dated observations, not permanent constants.
endlegend
@enduml
